{comment: Foster wrote it in 9/8 (three beats of three eighths). Here it is strummed as a slow 3/4 waltz, two bars of 3/4 for each 9/8 bar. Each opening line is I-V7, V7-I as in his 1864 score.}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
[G]Beautiful dreamer, [D7]wake unto me,
[D7]Starlight and dewdrops are [G]waiting for thee;
[G]Sounds of the rude world [D7]heard in the day,
[D7]Lull'd by the moonlight have [G]all pass'd away!
[D7]Beautiful dreamer, [G]queen of my song,
[D7]List while I woo thee with [G]soft melody;
[G]Gone are the cares of [C]life's busy [G]throng,
[Am]Beautiful dreamer, [D7]awake unto [G]me!
[Am]Beautiful dreamer, [D7]awake unto [G]me!
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
[G]Beautiful dreamer, [D7]out on the sea
[D7]Mermaids are chaunting the [G]wild Lorelie;
[G]Over the streamlet [D7]vapors are borne,
[D7]Waiting to fade at the [G]bright coming morn.
[D7]Beautiful dreamer, [G]beam on my heart,
[D7]E'en as the morn on the [G]streamlet and sea;
[G]Then will all clouds of [C]sorrow de[G]part,
[Am]Beautiful dreamer, [D7]awake unto [G]me!
[Am]Beautiful dreamer, [D7]awake unto [G]me!
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: G}
